WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 22: The Gunfight at the O.K. Corral & The Earp Vendetta Ride

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:15.520
[Rebecca] In the late 19th century, the silver boonton of Tombstone, Arizona, was one of the wealthiest, wildest, and most dangerous places in North America.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.040
[Rebecca] Beneath its bustling saloons and opera houses lay millions of dollars in silver bullion.

5
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.360
[Rebecca] And on its dusty streets, two irreconcilable factions collided.

6
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.360
[Rebecca] Northern Republican lawmen and Southern Confederate cattle rustlers, known simply as the Cowboys.

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:42.560
[Rebecca] On a freezing afternoon in October 1881, 30 seconds of gunfire in a narrow alley near the OK Corral sparked the most famous shootout in American frontier history.

8
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:52.400
[Rebecca] But the 32nd gunfight was only the opening chapter in a brutal cycle of ambush, murder, and federal posse vengeance known as the Earp Vendetta Ride.

9
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.200
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:23.920
[George] Following Ed Skifelin's massive discovery of silver veins in 1877, the town swelled to more than 10,000 residents, boasting luxury hotels, French restaurants, 16 saloons, and the famous Bird Cage Theater, which operated 24 hours a day.

11
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.680
[George] Yet beneath the glittering surface of silver prosperity, Tombstone was deeply fractured.

12
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:39.600
[George] The business owners and mine managers were largely Northern Republicans who demanded strict law, order, and municipal firearm bans to protect their capital investments.

13
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:52.600
[George] In sharp contrast, the surrounding rural valleys were dominated by an armed confederacy of cattle ranchers, rustlers and outlaws who wore red silk sashes and proudly called themselves the cowboys.

14
00:01:52.600 --> 00:02:08.920
[George] Led by cattle rustler Old Man Clanton and his hot-tempered sons Ike and Billy, along with brothers Tom and Frank McLaurie, the cowboys smuggled stolen Mexican cattle across the border, terrorized stagecoaches, and despised municipal authorities.

15
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.440
[George] Into this volatile environment stepped the Earp brothers.

16
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:21.400
[George] Virgil, a battle-hardened Union Army veteran appointed as tombstone town marshal, Wyatt, an imposing former Buffalo hunter and deputy U.S.

17
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.960
[George] Marshal, and younger brother Morgan.

18
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:36.520
[George] Joining them was Wyatt's loyal, sharp shooting companion, John Henry Doc Holiday, a tubercular Georgia dentist turned gambler with a razor-sharp wit and a lethal reputation with a revolver.

19
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:44.040
[George] Tensions reached an explosive climax on the freezing afternoon of Wednesday, October 26, 1881.

20
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:55.480
[George] For two days, Ike Clanton had been drinking heavily in tombstone saloons, openly carrying revolvers in violation of town ordinances and boasting that he would kill the Earps on site.

21
00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:04.040
[George] Armed with reports that the Clantons and McLorys were gathering weapons near Fremont Street, Town Marshal Virgil Earp decided to disarm them.

22
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:13.080
[George] Virgil deputized his brothers Wyatt and Morgan, handing a double-barreled ten-gauge shotgun to Doc Holiday to conceal beneath his long overcoat.

23
00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:20.760
[George] Side by side, their black wool coats flapping in the icy wind, the four men marched down 4th Street toward Fremont Street.

24
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:35.560
[George] In a narrow, 15 foot wide vacant lot between Fly's photography studio and the Harwood House, just down the alley from the OK Corral, they came face to face with Ike and Billy Clanton, Frank and Tom McLaurie, and Billy Claiborne.

25
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:41.960
[George] Virgil drew his cane and shouted firmly, Throw up your hands, boys, I intend to disarm you.

26
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:48.320
[George] Instead, Frank McLaurie reached for his holstered Colt revolver, and Billy Clanton drew his pistol.

27
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.880
[George] Wyatt drew his weapon in a split second.

28
00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.640
[George] Thirty shots were fired in thirty terrifying seconds.

29
00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:02.160
[George] When the thick white clouds of black powder smoke cleared, Billy Clanton and both McLaurie brothers lay dying on the ground.

30
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:09.360
[George] Virgil was shot through the calf, Morgan was wounded across both shoulder blades, and Holiday was grazed on the hip.

31
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.080
[George] Wyatt Earp stood completely untouched.

32
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.760
[George] The thirty-second gunfight was not the end of the conflict.

33
00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.880
[George] It was the catalyst for an all-out guerrilla war.

34
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:29.440
[George] Although a local judge ruled after a month-long hearing that the Earps and Holiday had acted within their legal authority as law officers, the surviving cowboys swore bloody vengeance.

35
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:44.000
[George] On the rainy night of December 28, 1881, Virgil Earp was walking across 5th Street from the Oriental Saloon to his hotel when three cowboy assassins concealed in an unfinished building opened fire with double barreled shotguns.

36
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
[George] Virgil was struck multiple times, buckshot shattered his left elbow and ripped through his back.

37
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.840
[George] Although he survived, his left arm was permanently crippled.

38
00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.080
[George] Less than three months later, on the evening of March 18, 1882, tragedy struck again.

39
00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:10.480
[George] While Morgan Earp was playing a game of late night billiards with Bob Hatch at Campbell and Hatch's saloon, two assassins fired shots through the glass pane of the rear alley door.

40
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.400
[George] One bullet struck Morgan in the spine, mortally wounding him.

41
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:21.120
[George] He collapsed onto the greenfelt pool table and died within an hour, whispering his final words to Wyatt.

42
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.720
[George] I can't see a thing.

43
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.640
[George] Wyatt held his dying younger brother in his arms, his heart hardening into an unyielding resolve.

44
00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.560
[George] The law courts of Cochise County were powerless, corrupt, and paralyzed.

45
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:39.960
[George] Wyatt decided that if the law would not punish his brother's murderers, he would deliver justice himself.

46
00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:57.160
[George] Securing a federal deputy marshal's commission from the United States Marshal for Arizona, Wyatt assembled a hand-picked posse of deadly, loyal gunmen, including Doc Holiday, Warren Earp, Sherman McMaster, Turkey Creek Jack Johnson, and Texas Jack Vermilion.

47
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:07.720
[George] Their first mission was to safely escort the crippled Virgil and his wife Allie, along with Morgan's coffin, onto a train bound for the family home in Colton, California.

48
00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:14.760
[George] On March 20, 1882, the party stopped at the Tucson rail station during an evening layover.

49
00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:26.600
[George] While walking Virgil to his train compartment, Wyatt spotted two heavily armed cowboy assassins, Frank Stilwell and Ike Clanton, lying in ambush among the rail flatcars in the dark yard.

50
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:31.720
[George] Wyatt drew his shotgun, leveled his six-gun, and charged into the darkness.

51
00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:37.480
[George] Stilwell attempted to flee down the tracks, but Wyatt caught up to him along the gravel siding.

52
00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:47.080
[George] The next morning, rail workers found Frank Stilwell's body riddled with buckshot from a shotgun blast at point blank range, along with three revolver wounds.

53
00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:53.560
[George] Having avenged Morgan's death in the Tucson rail yard, Wyatt refused to wait for local warrants.

54
00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:06.600
[George] He and his federal posse boarded the next freight train back toward Tombstone, abandoned their rail cars, mounted fast horses, and vanished into the Arizona wilderness to begin the legendary Earp Vendetta ride.

55
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:19.920
[George] Over the next three weeks, Wyatt Earp and his posse rode across hundreds of miles of rugged desert canyons, alkaline flats, and pinecrested mountains, hunting down every cowboy involved in the ambushes of his brothers.

56
00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:28.320
[George] The defining engagement of the Vendetta Ride occurred on March 24, 1882, at Iron Springs in the Whetstone Mountains.

57
00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:42.320
[George] Seeking water for their horses, Wyatt and his men rode down a dry wash, completely unaware that a faction of nine heavily armed cowboys, led by the notorious outlaw, Curly Bill Brocius, was camped near the springs.

58
00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:50.400
[George] As the Earps approached within fifty yards, the cowboys leaped from the brush and opened a furious barrage with Winchesters and shotguns.

59
00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.920
[George] Wyatt's posse retreated to cover, but Wyatt dismounted, refusing to flee.

60
00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:10.160
[George] With bullets tearing through the lapels of his coat and splintering the pommel of his saddle, Wyatt marched steadily forward through the hail of fire, cocked both barrels of his ten-gauge shotgun, and fired directly at Curly Bill Brocius.

61
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:18.160
[George] The buckshot blast struck Brocius squarely in the chest, cutting him in half and killing the notorious cowboy leader instantly.

62
00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:28.160
[George] Wyatt then emptied his revolver at the fleeing outlaws, climbed back onto his terrified horse, and led his men safely out of the ambush without losing a single man.

63
00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:41.280
[George] By mid-April 1882, having killed Curly Bill Brocious, Frank Stilwell, and Johnny Barnes, and having driven the Clanton faction out of southeastern Arizona, Wyatt concluded his vendetta.

64
00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:54.480
[George] Realizing that territorial warrants for murder were issued against him by cowboy-friendly sheriffs, Wyatt and his posse rode out of Arizona across the desert into Colorado, where the governor refused all extradition requests.

65
00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:05.960
[George] Doc Holliday's health deteriorated rapidly, and he passed away peacefully from tuberculosis five years later in a sanitarium in Glenwood Springs, Colorado, at the age of 36.

66
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:22.280
[George] Wyatt lived a long, colorful life, mining gold in Alaska, running saloons in San Diego, and eventually settling in Los Angeles, where he befriended early Hollywood film directors and advised young Western actors like Tom Mix and John Wayne.

67
00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:33.320
[George] When Wyatt died in 1929 at the age of 80, he had never suffered a single bullet wound in his entire frontier career, despite participating in dozens of shootouts.

68
00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:45.480
[George] The thirty seconds of gunsmoke in Tombstone and the relentless ride across the desert had forged an indelible myth, transforming a complex frontier lawman into the ultimate symbol of Western retribution.

69
00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:59.080
[George] The thirty seconds of gunfire in Tombstone and the bloody trail of the Vendetta Ride transformed Wyatt Earp into an eternal archetype of American justice, yet the debate over his true character continues to captivate historians.

70
00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:11.640
[George] While Eastern dime novels and early Western films celebrated Wyatt as an immaculate, fearless paragon of frontier law, local contemporary records paint a far more complex portrait of a pragmatic.

71
00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:20.200
[George] Battle-hardened frontiersman operating in a legal vacuum where the formal institutions of government were thoroughly subverted by outlaw syndicates.

72
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:33.320
[George] The coroner's inquest following the gunfight and the month long hearing before Judge Wells Spicer generated thousands of pages of conflicting testimony that remain the most intensely studied legal transcripts in the American West.

73
00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:43.240
[George] Today, the silver mines of Tombstone have long since flooded with subterranean water, but the town thrives as a living museum of frontier history.

74
00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:57.440
[George] Where millions of visitors walk along Fremont Street and stand in the narrow dirt alleyway where black powder smoke once blotted out the desert sky, drawn by the timeless legend of four men who refuse to yield to outlaw terror.

75
00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:07.840
[Rebecca] The gunfight at the OK Corral and the Earp Vendetta Ride represent the brutal, uncompromising collision between frontier lawlessness and civilization in territorial Arizona.

76
00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:13.360
[Rebecca] It remains a legendary saga of brotherhood, revenge, and unflinching frontier courage.

77
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:16.240
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

78
00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.440
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

79
00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:23.680
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we explore the bizarre military experiment that brought camels to the American desert.

80
00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.080
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

